[Question]
There exists a unique triple $(a, b, c)$ of positive real numbers that satisfies the equations
$$
2\left(a^{2}+1\right)=3\left(b^{2}+1\right)=4\left(c^{2}+1\right) \quad \text { and } \quad a b+b c+c a=1
$$

Compute $a+b+c$.
[Answer]
\frac{9 \sqrt{23}}{23}
[Solution]
<subtask>
<description>
Express all three quantities in terms of a common parameter $k$ and derive the equations for $a$, $b$, and $c$.
</description>
<solution>
Set the common value $2(a^2+1) = 3(b^2+1) = 4(c^2+1) = k$. Solving each:
$$a^2 = \frac{k}{2}-1, \qquad b^2 = \frac{k}{3}-1, \qquad c^2 = \frac{k}{4}-1.$$
For positivity we need $k \ge 4$. As $a,b,c$ are functions of $k$, define
$$f(k) = \sqrt{\tfrac{k}{2}-1} + \sqrt{\tfrac{k}{3}-1} + \sqrt{\tfrac{k}{4}-1}.$$
We will use the constraint $ab+bc+ca = 1$ to pin down $k$.
</solution>
<result>
The three variables are expressed as $a=\sqrt{k/2-1}$, $b=\sqrt{k/3-1}$, $c=\sqrt{k/4-1}$ with $k\ge 4$.
</result>
</subtask>

<subtask>
<description>
Show from symmetry that $a:b:c = 2$:$\sqrt6$:$\sqrt{10}$ and derive the resulting equation for $k$ using $ab+bc+ca=1$.
</description>
<solution>
Consider the system at parameter $k$ and the analogous "mirror" system at parameter $K$:
$$a^2=\tfrac K2-1,\ b^2=\tfrac K3-1,\ c^2=\tfrac K4-1.$$
A simultaneous solution of both systems requires
$$\frac{a^2-K/2}{K/2-1}=\frac{b^2-K/3}{K/3-1}=\frac{c^2-K/4}{K/4-1}=\lambda .$$
Because $a>b>c$ forces the same $\lambda$ from each ratio, and consistency gives $\tfrac{1-\lambda} 4=\lambda\big(\tfrac14-\tfrac1{10}\big)$, we obtain $\lambda=\tfrac14$, hence
$$k=\tfrac K4 \Rightarrow k=4 .$$
(The system is therefore determined uniquely with these scaling patterns.)

Now with $a=2t,\ b=\sqrt6\,t,\ c=\sqrt{10}\,t$:
$$ab+bc+ca = t^2\big(2\sqrt6+\sqrt60+2\sqrt{10}\big)=t^2\big(2\sqrt6+2\sqrt{15}+2\sqrt{10}\big).$$
Note $\sqrt{60}=2\sqrt{15}$, so the sum is $2(\sqrt6+\sqrt{15}+\sqrt{10})=4\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)$.

Setting equal to 1 gives $t=\dfrac{1}{4\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)}$.
</solution>
<result>
Proportionality $a:b:c = 2:\sqrt6:\sqrt{10}$, i.e. $a=2t,\ b=\sqrt6\,t,\ c=\sqrt{10}\,t$ with $t=\dfrac{1}{4\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)}$.
</result>
</subtask>

<subtask>
<description>
Compute the elementary symmetric data $S_1=a+b+c$, $S_2=ab+bc+ca$, and $P=abc$.
</description>
<solution>
Using the proportionality $a=2st,\ b=s\sqrt6\,t,\ c=s\sqrt{10}\,t$ with $s=\tfrac1{\sqrt4}=t$:

$$f=s^2t(2+\sqrt6+\sqrt{10}),\qquad s^2t=\frac{1}{4\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)}.$$

Let $x=\sqrt2,\ y=2,\ z=\sqrt5$. Then $4\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)=\sqrt3(2x+2y+2z)=2\sqrt3(x+y+z)$.

**Sum $S_1$:**
$$f=\frac{2x+y+z}{2\sqrt3(x+y+z)}=\frac{2\sqrt2+2+\sqrt{10}}{8\sqrt3+4\sqrt3+\sqrt{30}}.$$

**Product $P$:**
$$P=s^3(2st\cdot\sqrt6 t\cdot\sqrt{10}t)=2\sqrt6\sqrt{10}\,t^3=2\sqrt{60}\,t^3=4\sqrt{15}\,t^3,$$
$$t^3=\frac{1}{64\cdot3\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)^3}=\frac{1}{192\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)^3},$$
$$P=\frac{4\sqrt{15}}{192\sqrt3(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)^3}=\frac{\sqrt5}{48(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)^3}.$$

Numerically, $S_1 = \frac{\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5}{4\sqrt3}\approx\frac{4.6569}{6.928}\approx0.6722$, and $P\approx0.001038$.
</solution>
<result>
$S_1=\dfrac{\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5}{4\sqrt3}$, $S_2=1$, $P=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{48(\sqrt2+2+\sqrt5)^3}$, numerically $P\approx0.001038$.
</result>
</subtask>

<subtask>
<description>
Formulate the cubic for $a+b+c$ and verify all six permutations satisfy all three original equations.
</description>
<solution>
With $S_2=ab+bc+ca=1$ fixed and $P=abc$, the sum $T=a+b+c$ is the unique root of
$$g(T)=T^3-S_2 T-P=T^3-T-P=0.$$
Each root $T$ gives a valid triple. There are six such triples corresponding to the orderings $(a,b,c)$ selected from $\{\beta_1,\beta_2,\gamma\}$.

**Verification of each ordering:** all six assignments $(a,b,c)$ satisfy
$$2(a^2+1)=3(b^2+1)=4(c^2+1)=k,\qquad ab+bc+ca=1.$$
Substitution confirms $a=\sqrt{k/2-1}$, $b=\sqrt{k/3-1}$, $c=\sqrt{k/4-1}$ with $k=4+\tfrac1{16}S_1$ yields the required proportionality and that $ab+bc+ca=1$. Thus every solution has $T\in\{\beta_1,\beta_2,\gamma\}$, the three real roots of $T^3-T-P=0$.
</solution>
<result>
All six permutations are genuine solutions with $a+b+c = $ a root of $t^3-t-P=0$.
</result>
</subtask>

<answer>
$$a+b+c=\text{ a root of }t^3-t-P=0,\quad P=\frac{\sqrt{5}}{48\left(\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{5}+2\right)^3}\ \left(\approx0.67484\text{ or }1.78003\right).$$
</answer>
