<subtask>
<description>
Sum the three cyclic equations to derive a symmetric relation among $a, b, c$ and the sum $S = a+b+c$.
</description>
<solution>
Add the equations:
$$a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = (b+6) + (c+6) + (a+6) = (a+b+c) + 18.$$
Using the identity
$$a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S^2 - 2(ab+bc+ca),$$
and letting $S = a+b+c$, we obtain:
$$S^2 - 2(ab+bc+ca) = S + 18.$$

This gives one symmetric relation. We will combine it with further algebraic manipulations to pin down $S$.
</solution>
<result>
$$a^2+b^2+c^2 = S + 18, \quad\text{i.e., } S^2 - 2(ab+bc+ca) = S+18.$$
</result>
